Summary:
 A Report on the School Safety Impact of the 2012 Sandy Hook Elementary School Shooting
Schools are required to: Provide a strong security system for their students and parents. Provide appropriate resources for school staff to manage their schools and prevent dangerous weapons from becoming dangerous. Require schools to provide students and parents with the information needed to implement any new state laws that restrict or prohibit weapons.
For teachers, the Task Force Report recommends:
The use of a statewide standard of safety training at all state and local levels to address all types of school shootings;
Training local educators to prevent and respond quickly to any violent act by providing training and resources to local school districts;
Teachers in classrooms with guns, as well as teachers who are authorized to carry their own guns for school safety.

 Gov. Jan Brewer signed legislation which would have allowed businesses, churches or private individuals to deny service to gays. It was only allowed last week because the U.S. Supreme Court had denied same-sex marriage.


Arizona Gov. Jan Brewer signed legislation allowing businesses, churches, private individuals to deny service to gays. It was only allowed last week because the U.S. Supreme Court had denied same-sex marriage. Photo: Carlos Osorio, Associated Press

The law could also have made an impact on those who want to exclude gays from government services in Arizona — such as doctors' offices, a public safety agency or doctors' unions.

"When politicians get things wrong, it causes them to be wrong more often than not," said Republican Assemblyman Frank M. Dabakis, R-Tucson.

Rep. David Boren, R-El Centro, said he had sent an email about the bill to the governor during the campaign urging him not to sign or veto the bill. "I'd rather see the bad language," he wrote, but added it would be "more difficult to pass an amendment without seeing a negative impact on the Arizona people."

Mariana Smith, who runs the conservative Legislative Services Commission and was in the middle of a state Senate debate last week, said she still has faith in Brewer.

"I think there's so much at stake here," said Smith, a former state representative who lost a bid to replace Brewer as the state's governor in the 1999 Republican primary. "We've had so many failures. And the thing that really struck me was this little one piece. I couldn't think of anyone who believed in this whole thing that supported it when it came to discriminating against gays."

Democrats have criticized the bill as an attempt to limit same-sex marriage in Arizona. Republicans say the law is an attempt to stifle religious freedom, a claim often leveled at Republican senators who supported the measure.

"In order to move forward at all, the people are working at a very low level — I'm the top voter on the Senate Judiciary Committee," said Rep. Greg Aiello, R-Tucson.

The Republican governors who backed the bill were:

Sen. Jeff Flake of Arizona;

Sen. Brian Schatz of Hawaii;

Sen. John Thune of South Dakota;

Sen. John Barrasso of Wyoming;

Rep. Scott Garrett of Wyoming;

