 Trump made history as an antiwar and anti-war president Saturday, after he issued a directive to his first Cabinet secretaries to pull the U.S.-led coalition out of Syria with the end date set for February 29.
But he also declared the administration would pull out after his January 20th recess, making an abrupt pivot to the United States, as a sign of confidence that he will leave the post for the safety of his national security adviser, H.R. McMaster, who he has replaced in Trump's Cabinet.
In a December 20th letter to Trump, McMaster argued the Obama administration "is intent on the war," and not an "opposition party," in his assessment of the president.
In April, McMaster wrote to Trump that he "hadn't thought of that yet."
And earlier this month, McMaster told POLITICO that the White House is not ready to make a decision regarding withdrawing the troops, a move that he said would involve "significant time and effort."
In addition to Mattis, three other former Defense secretaries who previously served under him will now be under consideration at various level. They include Lt. Gen. Michael Hayden, now in his second year of service, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and Gen. Keith Kellogg, who retired last year.
The four officials who have joined them at the Department of Defense, are:
- Michael Chertoff, former National Security Advisor;
- Joseph M. Bedoya, senior deputy to the director of national intelligence;
- Michael D. Coats, former deputy general counsel of the Central Intelligence Agency;
- Daniel Pipes, senior deputy chief of staff for planning;
- Robert C. Wilson, deputy assistant national security adviser;
- Dan Coats, director of national intelligence; and
- Matthew M. Hahn, the former deputy assistant attorney general in charge of the National Security Division.
